        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 663,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 798,
            "height": 656,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "rotate_position turns a position about one of the three axes.\n\nGive it an x y z position and an angle in degrees; it sends the position rotated by that angle about the X, Y or Z axis, around the origin. It is the quick way to turn a tracked point into a different frame - to correct a sensor mounted at an angle, or to face a body forward before measuring it.\n\nA ROTATION FOLLOWS THE RIGHT-HAND RULE:\nPoint your right thumb along the chosen axis; positive angles turn the way your fingers curl. About Y, a point at 1 0 0 turned by 90 degrees ends at 0 0 -1.\n\nONE POSITION OR MANY:\nA single position of three numbers comes out as three numbers. An array of positions, one x y z per row, comes out with every row rotated. Anything else - a two-number point, say - is passed through UNCHANGED, and the node prints a warning the first time it happens.\n\nONLY THE POSITION TRIGGERS:\nChanging \"angle\" does not send anything by itself - the new angle is used on the next position that arrives. Changing \"axis\" does re-send the last position straight away. To sweep the angle over a fixed point, keep sending the point, as the demo does with tick.\n\nThe result is always float32.\n\nSYNTAX:\nrotate_position\n\nEXAMPLE:\nrotate_position\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nposition:\nAn x y z position, or an array of them. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nangle:\nThe rotation, in degrees.\n\naxis:\nX, Y or Z. Default Y - the vertical axis in most body data, so the default turns things about the vertical.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotated:\nThe rotated position or positions.\n\nRELATED:\nFor rotations about an arbitrary axis, or by a quaternion, use the quaternion nodes.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 790,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 640,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "rotate_position turns a position about one of the three axes.\n\nGive it an x y z position and an angle in degrees; it sends the position rotated by that angle about the X, Y or Z axis, around the origin. It is the quick way to turn a tracked point into a different frame - to correct a sensor mounted at an angle, or to face a body forward before measuring it.\n\nA ROTATION FOLLOWS THE RIGHT-HAND RULE:\nPoint your right thumb along the chosen axis; positive angles turn the way your fingers curl. About Y, a point at 1 0 0 turned by 90 degrees ends at 0 0 -1.\n\nONE POSITION OR MANY:\nA single position of three numbers comes out as three numbers. An array of positions, one x y z per row, comes out with every row rotated. Anything else - a two-number point, say - is passed through UNCHANGED, and the node prints a warning the first time it happens.\n\nONLY THE POSITION TRIGGERS:\nChanging \"angle\" does not send anything by itself - the new angle is used on the next position that arrives. Changing \"axis\" does re-send the last position straight away. To sweep the angle over a fixed point, keep sending the point, as the demo does with tick.\n\nThe result is always float32.\n\nSYNTAX:\nrotate_position\n\nEXAMPLE:\nrotate_position\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nposition:\nAn x y z position, or an array of them. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nangle:\nThe rotation, in degrees.\n\naxis:\nX, Y or Z. Default Y - the vertical axis in most body data, so the default turns things about the vertical.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrotated:\nThe rotated position or positions.\n\nRELATED:\nFor rotations about an arbitrary axis, or by a quaternion, use the quaternion nodes."
        },
